---
import DistaDocs from "../../../../components/DistaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const description =
  "Dista's actors: state on a worker of its own with methods called in order, handles passed to tasks and other actors, restarts when a worker dies, checkpoints to a directory or S3, and actors moving off a draining worker.";
---

<DistaDocs href="/s/dista/docs/actors" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Actors and checkpoints</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An actor is state that lives on one worker between calls. Its methods run one at a
    time, in the order they were made, from the driver, from tasks or from other actors.
    Checkpoints let it come back with its state when its worker goes away.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#actors">Actors</a>
    <a href="#handles">Calling from tasks</a>
    <a href="#restarts">Restarts</a>
    <a href="#checkpoints">Checkpoints</a>
    <a href="#moving">Draining an actor's worker</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="actors">Actors</h2>
  <p>
    An actor is a state type, the function that builds it, and methods that take it
    <code>mut</code>. Actors and methods go in <code>Runtime[...]</code> with the tasks:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={`from dista import Actor, Method, Runtime

@fieldwise_init
struct Counter(Movable):
    var total: Int

def new_counter(start: Int) -> Counter:
    return Counter(start)

def add(mut c: Counter, n: Int) -> Int:
    c.total += n
    return c.total

comptime CounterActor = Actor[Counter, Int, new_counter]
comptime Add = Method[CounterActor, Int, Int, add]

var rt = Runtime[CounterActor, Add, Square](workers=2)
var c = rt.create[CounterActor](0)
var n = rt.call[Add](c, 5)            # ObjectRef[Int]
print(rt.take(rt.remote[Square](n)))  # 25
rt.kill(c)                            # after its earlier calls; frees the worker`} />
  <p>
    <code>create</code> returns at once; calls made before the actor is up wait for it,
    and if its constructor raises, every call fails with that error. Each actor gets a
    worker of its own, which runs no tasks while it hosts it. An actor created when every
    worker already hosts one waits for a worker to join or another actor to be killed. A
    method's result is an <code>ObjectRef</code> like a task's, so a task can take it as
    input. Calling a method on the wrong actor's handle is a compile error.
  </p>

  <h2 id="handles">Calling from tasks</h2>
  <p>
    A handle is a value, so a task or another actor can call the actor too. Declare the
    task with <code>CtxTask</code>, or the method with <code>CtxMethod</code>, and its
    function gets a <code>Client</code> to call through. A <code>CtxMethod</code>'s
    function takes the state, then the client, then its argument.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={`from dista import ActorHandle, Client, CtxTask

def bump(mut c: Client, h: ActorHandle[CounterActor]) raises -> Int:
    return c.take(c.call[Add](h, 1))

comptime Bump = CtxTask[ActorHandle[CounterActor], Int, bump]
var n = rt.remote[Bump](c)   # a task calling the actor`} />
  <p>
    Calls from tasks go through the head, which keeps their order. A call blocks the task
    or actor making it until the answer comes. Tasks never run on an actor's worker, so a
    task can't wait on itself, but a cycle of actor calls, one actor calling another that
    calls it back before answering, deadlocks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="restarts">Restarts</h2>
  <p>
    When an actor's worker goes away, the head places the actor on another worker,
    builds it again from its last checkpoint, or its constructor if it has none, and runs
    every call since then again, in order. Each call is answered once. A call may run
    twice on the actor's state, so a method should be safe to repeat; that's the price of
    not losing calls.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoints">Checkpoints</h2>
  <p>
    A checkpoint is the actor's state through the codec, written to
    <code>DISTA_CHECKPOINT_URI</code>: a local directory, a <code>file://</code> one, or
    anything objectstore writes to, such as <code>s3://bucket/prefix</code>. A local
    directory only helps if the node that restarts the actor sees it too, so a cluster
    needs a shared volume or object storage. Without the variable, checkpoints fail. Ask
    for one every so many calls, or when it suits:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={`var c = rt.create[CounterActor](0, checkpoint_every=100)
...
var calls = rt.take(rt.checkpoint(c))   # written; covers this many calls`} />
  <p>
    A restart then replays only the calls since the checkpoint, and the head stops keeping
    the ones it covers. A state type that implements <code>Codec</code> decides what it
    saves: a cache can save nothing, a loaded model its path. Checkpoints also carry an
    actor across a head restart: the new head restores it from its newest checkpoint, and
    its callers send again the calls no checkpoint includes. Killing an actor deletes its
    checkpoints.
  </p>

  <h2 id="moving">Draining an actor's worker</h2>
  <p>
    A worker that <a href="/s/dista/docs/cluster#draining">drains</a> moves its actor
    rather than losing it. The head holds the actor's new calls, asks its worker for a
    checkpoint, restores the actor on another worker from it, and sends it the held
    calls: no call runs twice, and it doesn't count as a restart. Without
    <code>DISTA_CHECKPOINT_URI</code> that checkpoint fails, and the actor restarts when
    the worker leaves, replaying its calls, as when a worker dies.
  </p>
</DistaDocs>
